menu "Free Harvest Adapter"

choice HR_BOARD
    prompt "Target board"
    default HR_BOARD_DEVKITC
    help
        Which board the firmware is built for. The protocol, web UI, MQTT
        and capture log are identical on every board; the choice only adds
        the peripherals a particular board carries. The default keeps the
        build exactly as it always was for a bare ESP32-S3 DevKitC.

config HR_BOARD_DEVKITC
    bool "ESP32-S3 DevKitC-1 (no display, no LED, no button)"

config HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    bool "LilyGo T-Dongle-S3 (0.96\" ST7735 LCD, APA102 LED, BOOT button)"
    help
        USB-A stick with an 80x160 colour LCD, one RGB LED and one button.
        Note the board has a single USB port that TinyUSB takes over for the
        dryer, so the serial console is only reachable on UART0 (GPIO43/44,
        the QWIIC connector on recent revisions) or through /api/log.

endchoice

config HR_DISPLAY
    bool "Status display on the on-board LCD"
    depends on HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    default y
    help
        Show the cycle phase, temperature, vacuum and connection state on the
        LCD. Purely an output: nothing on the display path can send a frame
        to the dryer.

config HR_LED
    bool "Mirror the state on the RGB LED"
    depends on HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    default y
    help
        Colour = phase, blink/breathe = attention needed. Brightness is capped
        at 30 % because the LED sits inside a closed case next to the antenna.

config HR_BUTTON
    bool "Use the BOOT button to switch screens / toggle the backlight"
    depends on HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    default y
    help
        Short press: dismiss an alert, or cycle main -> info -> raw frame.
        Long press (1.5 s): backlight and LED off/on. The button never sends
        anything to the dryer - a stray press on a stick protruding from the
        machine must not be able to start or stop a batch.

config HR_DISPLAY_ROTATE_180
    bool "Rotate the display 180 degrees (USB plug on the right)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY
    default y
    help
        USB port of the Harvest Right dryer is on the right side; rotate so
        the screen reads upright when plugged in. Disable this for a port
        on the left (the vendor sketch's orientation). Only the panel's
        MADCTL changes; the frame buffer, fonts, button and LED are
        unaffected.

config HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    bool "Dim, then switch off the backlight when nothing happens"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY
    default y
    help
        The stick sits in the dryer around the clock. After HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S
        without an event the backlight ramps down to HR_DISPLAY_DIM_PCT
        (the screen keeps updating); after HR_DISPLAY_OFF_S the backlight
        and the panel are switched off. Events that wake the screen: a
        button press, the dryer changing phase (start, freeze -> dry,
        complete), an alert, the USB link dropping or returning, Wi-Fi or
        MQTT state changes. Plain telemetry updates (temperature, vacuum,
        elapsed time) do not, or the screen would never dim during a run.
        The IPS panel has no burn-in; this is about heat, backlight life
        and glare at night. Disable to keep the old always-on behaviour.

config HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S
    int "Dim after (seconds of inactivity)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    range 5 86400
    default 30

config HR_DISPLAY_OFF_S
    int "Switch the screen off after (seconds of inactivity, 0 = never)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    range 0 604800
    default 300
    help
        Counted from the last event, like HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S, so it should be
        larger than that; a value at or below HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S skips the
        DIM stage. 0 keeps the screen dimmed but never off. While the
        setup access point is open the screen never goes off: the user is
        reading the network name and address off it. When the screen is
        off, the first button press only wakes it (nothing else happens).

config HR_DISPLAY_DIM_PCT
    int "Dimmed backlight (%)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    range 1 99
    default 10

config HR_DISPLAY_ALERT_KEEP_ON
    bool "Keep the screen at full brightness while an alert is showing"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    default y
    help
        An alert that has not been acknowledged (link lost mid-run, dryer
        diagnostics, bad frames...) holds the screen ACTIVE. Disable to let
        it dim; it never goes off while an alert is active either way, and
        an acknowledged alert (status-bar "!") allows DIM but not OFF.

config HR_LED_DIM_WITH_DISPLAY
    bool "Dim the RGB LED as well when the screen is off"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM && HR_LED
    default y
    help
        With the screen off the LED keeps mirroring the machine state, but
        capped at a low, still visible level (5 %) instead of its normal
        10-30 %. Disable to keep the LED at full pattern brightness.

menu "Bench debugging over USB (not for the dryer)"

config HR_USB_LOG_CDC
    bool "Mirror the ESP log to a second USB CDC port (CDC1)"
    depends on TINYUSB_CDC_COUNT = 2
    default n
    help
        With CONFIG_TINYUSB_CDC_COUNT=2 the stick enumerates as two serial
        ports: CDC0 stays the dryer protocol exactly as before, CDC1 carries
        every ESP_LOG line, non-blocking (lines are dropped, and counted,
        while nobody reads the port). For a bench with a computer as the
        USB host: boards whose only USB port TinyUSB owns have no other
        live console. Do NOT ship this to the dryer - its host stack has
        only ever been seen with a single-CDC or CDC+MSC device.

config HR_USB_TRACE_RX
    bool "Log every raw USB RX chunk (length + first bytes)"
    default n
    help
        One INFO line per chunk the CDC RX callback drains, with the length
        and the first 32 bytes (printable as-is, the rest as \xNN), BEFORE
        the frame parser sees them. Answers "does anything at all arrive
        from the dryer" even when nothing parses. Noisy; bench builds only.

endmenu

config HR_AP_SSID
    string "Setup access-point SSID"
    default "HR-Adapter-Setup"
    help
        SSID broadcast when no WiFi credentials are stored (or the stored
        network can't be joined). Connect to it to reach the setup page.

config HR_AP_PASSWORD
    string "Setup access-point password (blank = open)"
    default ""
    help
        Password for the setup AP. Leave blank for an open network. If set,
        must be at least 8 characters (WPA2 requirement).

config HR_WIFI_NOIP_DHCP_RESTART_S
    int "No-IP watchdog: restart the DHCP client after (seconds)"
    range 5 600
    default 15
    help
        A station can stay associated - beacons, RSSI, no disconnect event -
        after its DHCP lease expires and the router stops answering renewals;
        lwIP then sits at 0.0.0.0 broadcasting DISCOVER. Once the station
        has been associated without an address for this long, the DHCP
        client is stopped and started so it begins from a clean state.
        Must be shorter than HR_WIFI_NOIP_RECONNECT_S to matter. The status
        (web UI, /api/state, the dryer's WIFIINFO) reports "no-ip" instead
        of "connected" after 5 s regardless (12 s for the first address
        after a join, where DHCP starts from scratch and a weak link has
        been seen to need 8 s).

config HR_WIFI_NOIP_RECONNECT_S
    int "No-IP watchdog: leave and rejoin the network after (seconds)"
    range 10 3600
    default 45
    help
        If restarting DHCP did not bring an address back, the station
        disconnects and reconnects so both the association and DHCP start
        afresh. While the address stays missing the wait doubles on each
        rejoin (45, 90, 180 s ...) up to HR_WIFI_NOIP_RECONNECT_MAX_S, and
        resets as soon as an address is obtained. The setup AP is NOT
        re-opened by these rejoins.

config HR_WIFI_NOIP_RECONNECT_MAX_S
    int "No-IP watchdog: longest wait between rejoins (seconds)"
    range 10 7200
    default 360
    help
        Cap for the doubling back-off above. Bounds how often a network
        that never hands out an address is rejoined: at most once per this
        many seconds, forever - the adapter is powered by the dryer and
        must find its way back on its own.

config HR_WIFI_GW_PROBE_S
    int "Link watchdog: probe the gateway every (seconds, 0 = off)"
    range 0 600
    default 20
    help
        On a weak signal a station can go on hearing the router's beacons
        (sent slow and loud) after its own frames stop getting across. The
        driver stays associated, the DHCP lease stays valid, the status
        says "connected" with an address - and nothing is reachable, until
        the router drops the station (minutes) or the lease runs out
        (hours). While an address is held the adapter sends the gateway an
        ARP request this often; a router that is there at all answers it.
        0 turns the probe off.

config HR_WIFI_GW_PROBE_MISSES
    int "Link watchdog: unanswered probes in a row before rejoining"
    range 1 20
    default 3
    help
        This many probes without an answer in a row and the link is
        reported as "unreachable" (not connected, like "no-ip") and the
        network is left and rejoined so the router sees the station afresh.
        With the 20 s default interval, 3 misses means a dead link is acted
        on within about a minute. The misses required double on each rejoin
        that still gets no answer (up to 16x), and reset at the first answer,
        so a router that is really gone is not rejoined every minute for
        hours. The setup AP is NOT re-opened by these rejoins.

config HR_ACK_PAYLOAD
    string "GOTIT ack payload (UNVERIFIED)"
    default "ESP32ADAPTER"
    help
        Value sent in the GOTIT acknowledgement. The genuine adapter's
        payload is not yet known - capture it with a USB sniffer and set it
        here (or leave for later; the decoding UI works regardless).

config HR_COMPAT_644170
    bool "Handshake for dryer firmware 6.0.644170 (default at first boot)"
    default n
    help
        Send "UNIQUE lH" instead of a bare "UNIQUE", and re-send FDNAME /
        REQCFG / STATUS on every 15 s heartbeat until each is answered -
        the genuine adapter's observed behaviour.

        A dryer on 6.0.644170 answers a bare UNIQUE with UID and then never
        sends anything else (no SNM, CFG, STAT). That build adds an
        adapter-mode byte that its UNIQUE handler sets only when the frame
        carries "lH"; 6.0.641041 has no such byte and works either way.
        Verified harmless on 6.0.641041.

        This is only the value used until the switch is changed at runtime
        (Settings > Debug, or POST /api/compat), which is stored in NVS.

config HR_BATCH_HISTORY
    bool "Batch history: read the dryer's own batch CSV logs (FDFILES / FILEREAD)"
    default y
    help
        A client for the dryer's file protocol - FDFILES to list, FILEREAD
        to read, FDFILELIST / FDFILEBLOCK replies - so the web panel can show
        the per-batch CSV logs the dryer keeps itself (shelf, vacuum, phases
        and the Room-J18 ambient thermocouple, which no live frame carries).
        Adds GET/POST /api/dryer/files* and a "Batch history" page.

        Reads only: both verbs are in the SAFE allow-list and nothing here
        can start, stop or change a run. Costs about 4 KB of static RAM
        (block side buffer, list cache) plus 8 KB of heap while a file is
        being read; files are streamed to the browser, nothing is stored.

        Confirmed live 2026-09-17 on a 6.0.644170 machine (docs/30): list,
        read, checksums, a 276 KB batch log in 55 s at ~5 KB/s with the
        dryer's 15 s STAT cadence untouched.

config HR_BATCH_HISTORY_DEFAULT_ON
    bool "Batch history: switched on at first boot"
    depends on HR_BATCH_HISTORY
    default n
    help
        Value of the runtime switch (Settings > Batch history, or POST
        /api/dryer/files/enable, kept in NVS) until the owner changes it.
        Off: the code is present but sends nothing to the dryer until the
        switch is flipped. The T-Dongle-S3 defaults turn it on; the generic
        build stays off so an upstream cherry-pick changes nothing on the
        wire by itself. Either way a read is refused while a batch runs
        unless the request says force=1.

config HR_HTTP_LOG_TO_UART
    bool "Also echo received frames to the UART console"
    default y

config HR_LOG_RING_KB
    int "In-app log ring (/api/log) size, KB"
    range 4 128
    default 32
    help
        Static DRAM reserved for the in-app copy of the ESP-IDF log that
        /api/log serves. Lines are stored packed (a line costs its own
        length plus two bytes), so 32 KB keeps roughly 500 lines of the
        ~65-character average - a boot, the handshake and several minutes
        of a busy link. Boards without PSRAM that carry other RAM users can
        turn this down; the ring only ever drops its oldest lines.

config HR_CAPTURE_RAW
    bool "Capture log: keep every frame verbatim (no repeat collapsing)"
    default n
    help
        By default consecutive identical frames (an idle dryer's REQINFO
        every 10 s) are collapsed into one "repeat ... xN" summary line to
        save flash. Enable this when recording traffic for protocol work,
        where the exact cadence of every frame matters more than space.
        On a 16 MB board the 11.9 MB capture partition holds several days
        of raw traffic even so.

endmenu
